  <!-- ── Top 10 Rejection Reasons ─────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Top 10 Reasons for Non-Lucrative Visa Rejection</h2>
        <p>These are the most common rejection reasons identified by applicants and immigration professionals. Each is fixable with the right approach.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__rejection-reasons">
        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">1</span> Insufficient or Unclear Income Proof</h4>
          <p>The consulate needs clear evidence that you meet the <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-income-requirements/">income threshold</a> (typically €27,792/year for a single person, but this varies by consulate and your support obligations). Bank statements must show regular income deposits, pension payments, or investment income. Vague or irregular deposits raise red flags. Consulates want to see consistent, declared, taxable income over a defined period — usually the last 6–12 months.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">2</span> Wrong Health Insurance (Copay Policy)</h4>
          <p>This is one of the most common rejection reasons. Spanish consulates require private <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-health-insurance/">health insurance with NO copay clauses</a>. If your policy requires you to pay a percentage or fixed amount per visit, the consulate will reject it. Many standard international health insurance policies include copays — you need to verify yours explicitly states "no copay" or "100% coverage" for doctor visits and hospitalization. This is fixable in days once you know the issue.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">3</span> Incomplete or Missing Documents</h4>
          <p>The <a href="/non-lucrative-visa-documents/">checklist varies by consulate</a>, but typically includes: criminal record certificate, medical certificate, birth certificate, marriage certificate (if applicable), divorce decree, passport copies, educational certificates, accommodation proof, and financial documents. Even one missing or incomplete document can trigger rejection. Your consulate's requirements are specific — follow their checklist exactly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">4</span> Expired Documents</h4>
          <p>Criminal records, medical certificates, and some financial documents have limited validity periods. If any document is outdated or nearing expiration, the consulate will reject the application. Always submit documents dated within the consulate's required timeframe — typically 3–6 months before application for medical certificates, and 6–12 months for criminal records. Check your consulate's specific requirements.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">5</span> Missing Apostille on Key Documents</h4>
          <p>Documents from your home country must be apostilled (officially certified) to be valid in Spain. The apostille is a special seal that confirms the document's authenticity. If your criminal record, medical certificate, birth certificate, or educational documents lack apostilles, the consulate will reject them. Apostilles typically take 4–8 weeks to process, so plan ahead.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">6</span> Missing or Poor-Quality Sworn Translations</h4>
          <p>Documents in English must be translated into Spanish by an official translator (traductor jurado). The translation must be certified. Without sworn translations, documents are not legally acceptable. Some consulates are stricter than others about translation quality — if your translations are poorly formatted or lack proper certification marks, the consulate may reject them. Use a reputable sworn translator.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">7</span> Criminal Record or Other Legal Issues</h4>
          <p>If your criminal record shows convictions (including minor offences in some cases), drug-related charges, or recent crimes, the consulate may reject your application. Some consulates will not approve applicants with any criminal history; others evaluate on a case-by-case basis. If this is your rejection reason, consult an immigration lawyer — you may need to seek a pardon or special waiver depending on the offence and consulate.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">8</span> Medical Certificate Issues</h4>
          <p>The medical certificate must be from a licensed physician and must not show conditions that consulates consider incompatible with residence (e.g., untreated infectious diseases, severe mental health conditions that affect independent living). If your medical certificate is unclear, outdated, or raises health concerns, the consulate may request additional medical evidence or reject the application. Ensure your doctor completes the certificate thoroughly and clearly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">9</span> Insufficient Accommodation Proof</h4>
          <p>You must prove you have suitable accommodation in Spain. This can be a rental contract, property deed, or a letter from someone allowing you to live in their property. The accommodation must be yours exclusively or with your family — you cannot simply list a hotel booking. If your accommodation proof is unclear or temporary, the consulate may reject it. Secure a proper rental or property before submitting your application.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__rejection-card">
          <h4><span class="msv__rejection-card-number">10</span> Poor Presentation or Formatting of Financial Documents</h4>
          <p>Bank statements must be clear, recent (typically from the last 1–3 months), and show your name and account number clearly. If statements are difficult to read, inconsistently formatted, or lack essential information, the consulate may reject them. Some banks provide translations in English or Spanish — request these. Ensure all financial documents are professional, complete, and easy to verify.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Keep your rejection letter.</strong> If your application was rejected, the consulate should provide written notification. This document explains the reason(s) for rejection. Keep it safe — you'll need it for any appeal or reapplication.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

