  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>The Appeal Process (Recurso de Alzada)</h2>
        <p>If you choose to appeal rather than reapply, here's how the formal legal process works and what to expect.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; color: var(--navy);">How the Appeal Process Works</h3>

      <div style="background: var(--white); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1rem;">Filing an Appeal</h4>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">An appeal must be filed within <strong>one month</strong> of receiving your rejection letter. You submit:</p>
        <ul style="color: var(--muted);">
          <li>A formal appeal letter (recurso de alzada) explaining why you believe the rejection was incorrect</li>
          <li>Your original rejection letter</li>
          <li>Any new or corrected documents addressing the consulate's concerns</li>
          <li>Proof of submission (often required)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">Submit the appeal to the consulate's appeals office (usually the same consulate that rejected you). Some consulates accept appeals by email; others require certified mail or in-person submission. Check your consulate's specific procedures.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--white); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1rem;">What Happens After Filing</h4>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">After you submit your appeal:</p>
        <ul style="color: var(--muted);">
          <li>The consulate's administrative office reviews your case (usually a different person than the original decision-maker)</li>
          <li>They assess whether your appeal addresses the rejection reason</li>
          <li>They decide whether to uphold the original decision or overturn it</li>
          <li>This process typically takes 1–6 months (varies significantly by consulate)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"><strong>Important:</strong> The consulate is not obligated to change its decision, even if you've corrected the documents. Appeals are successful only if the consulate determines the original decision was procedurally or substantively wrong.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--white); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 2rem;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1rem;">Success Rates and Realistic Expectations</h4>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">Appeal success rates typically range from 30–50%, depending on the reason for rejection and the specific consulate. Appeals are most successful when:</p>
        <ul style="color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1rem;">
          <li>The consulate made a procedural error in reviewing your application</li>
          <li>You've corrected the specific issue and can demonstrate it clearly</li>
          <li>The consulate is generally friendly to NLV applicants</li>
          <li>Your appeal letter is well-written and professional (consider having a lawyer review it)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">Appeals are less successful when consulates have already made a firm determination. For this reason, many applicants find reapplication faster and more successful.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚖️</span>
        <p><strong>Consider legal support for appeals.</strong> An appeal is a formal legal process. If your rejection is complex or involves criminal record or medical issues, consider consulting an immigration lawyer or a professional service. The cost is often worth the improved chances of success.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

