        <details>
          <summary>What are the main reasons for Non-Lucrative Visa rejection?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The top rejection reasons include: insufficient or unclear income proof, private health insurance with copay clauses (consulates require no-copay policies), incomplete or expired documents, missing apostilles or sworn translations, criminal record issues, medical certificate problems, insufficient accommodation proof, poor document presentation, and issues with dependent income requirements. Each can be identified and fixed before reapplication.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I appeal a Non-Lucrative Visa rejection in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. You can file a formal appeal (recurso de alzada) within one month of rejection with the same consulate or the relevant Spanish administrative authority. The appeal must include the rejection letter and any new or corrected documents addressing the consulate's concerns. Success rates for appeals vary by consulate and reason for rejection — appeals are most successful when you've identified and fixed the specific issue. Many applicants prefer to reapply instead, as the process is often faster.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to reapply for the Non-Lucrative Visa after rejection?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After identifying and fixing the issues, most applicants can reapply within 2–8 weeks, depending on how quickly they can obtain corrected documents. If you rejected due to a missing apostille or translation, plan 4–8 weeks for processing. If the issue is a health insurance policy, you can arrange that within days. With My Spanish Visa's guidance, the entire reapplication process typically takes 4–12 weeks from rejection to resubmission.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is a formal appeal (recurso de alzada) and should I file one?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>A recurso de alzada is a formal legal objection to the consulate's decision, filed within one month of rejection. You submit the appeal letter along with any new documentation to the consulate's appeals office. Success depends on whether you've fixed the underlying issue — if the consulate rejected you for incomplete documents, you must submit complete, corrected documents with the appeal. Some consulates favor reapplication over appeals. Consult an immigration lawyer or professional service to decide which path is best for your specific situation.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How can My Spanish Visa help after my Non-Lucrative Visa is rejected?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>My Spanish Visa provides a professional review of your rejected application to identify the exact reasons for rejection, detailed guidance on document correction and recompilation, support in obtaining missing apostilles or updated translations, reapplication strategy and submission, and appeal guidance if that's your preferred path. Our review typically identifies 2–5 specific fixes that improve your chances of approval on reapplication. We've helped hundreds of applicants turn rejections into approvals.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the most common document reason for NLV rejection?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Incorrect health insurance is one of the most common document reasons — specifically, insurance with copayments, incomplete Spain coverage, or wrong format certificates. The second most common is financial evidence that doesn't clearly show the required monthly income — either too low, from an ineligible source, or poorly documented.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get my visa application fee back after a rejection?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. Consulate visa fees are non-refundable regardless of the outcome. Health insurance purchased for the application may have a cancellation/refund window — check the policy.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will an NLV rejection affect my tourist visa or future travel to Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>A rejected NLV application doesn't automatically affect your right to visit Spain as a tourist (within the 90/180 Schengen rules). However, a pattern of applications based on misrepresentation could affect future visa assessments. A single good-faith rejection that is subsequently reapplied for with correct documentation should not cause ongoing problems.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
